  <h1>The Ember Song</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 2 of 2</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/the-ember-song?page=1#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><span class="page-next disabled">Next &#8594;</span></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Rose learned them the way children learn things — not by understanding but by repetition, by the particular gift that children have for absorbing patterns without knowing what the patterns mean. She learned the sounds. She learned the rhythms. She did not learn the meaning, not yet, not in the way that would come later, when the words finally found the place in her that they were always meant to fill.</p>
<p>Evelyn died on a Thursday, in the early morning, before dawn. The family had known it was coming — the hospice nurse had told them it was a matter of days, and they had gathered, and they had waited, and they had done the things that families do in the long hours of a vigil that they know will end.</p>
<p>She died quietly. She had not spoken in two days, and she had not been fully conscious in longer than that, but when the moment came, she opened her eyes and looked at the room full of family and she smiled, and then she was gone.</p>
<p>The funeral was four days later. That was the custom. Four days to prepare, to notify, to allow the people who lived far away to travel. The family gathered in the old church — the same church where the song had been sung at every funeral for as long as anyone could remember — and the minister said the words, and the family sat in the front rows, and no one mentioned the song.</p>
<p>This was the custom too. The song was not for the funeral. The song was for the grave.</p>
<p>The grave was on a hill outside town, in the cemetery that the family had been using for generations. There was a section — just a section, a few rows of stones — that belonged to them, and Evelyn's stone was already in place, and the family stood around it in the cold February air, and the minister had finished speaking, and now there was nothing left but the song.</p>
<p>Catherine looked at Rose. Rose was five now — she had had a birthday in the month since Evelyn died, the way time keeps moving even when you want it to stop — and she was standing in her black coat with her hands in her pockets, looking at the grave with the expression of a child who is trying very hard to understand something that is too big for her.</p>
<p>"The fifth verse," Catherine said, quietly. "Remember?"</p>
<p>Rose nodded. She did not look at her mother. She was looking at the grave, at the stone, at the name carved in the stone.</p>
<p>She sang.</p>
<p>Her voice was a child's voice — thin, uncertain, the voice of someone who is not sure of themselves and is not afraid to show it. But the melody was right. The rhythm was right. The words, in the old language, came out of her mouth the way they had come out of Evelyn's mouth for eighty-seven years.</p>
<p><em>What the ember keeps, the ember gives back.</em></p>
<p>The family listened. Some of them knew the fifth verse. Some of them knew parts of the sixth and seventh. None of them knew the eighth through eleventh — those had been Evelyn's alone, and now they were gone, gone with her, returned to whatever place the words go.</p>
<p>But Rose knew the fifth. And she knew the melody. And she knew — though she could not have said why — that she would learn the rest. That she would have time. That the ember was still warm, and the fire was still lit, and this was not an ending but a passing.</p>
<p>She sang the verse again. The family joined in on the refrain.</p>
<p>The wind moved through the cemetery. The February sky was grey. The stone held its name.</p>
<p>And the ember, against all odds, stayed lit.</p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
